  "interpretation": "amphora-solvency-score ships a Hansen RED verdict built from 25 C parameter tables and an interaction radius R that carries no temperature argument, yet marine two-part epoxy-amine coatings are applied near 5 C and serviced near 40 C. Applying Hansen & Beerbower's own published temperature law (1971; zero fitted parameters) across that envelope: the 25 C baseline reproduces the shipped 67/75 headline exactly and the law implementation matches the published derivatives to 0.0 relative error. H0 (verdict invariance) is REFUTED: worst case 5 of 75 verdicts flip inside 5-40 C, with max |dRED| 0.392252. Where the refutation does and does not rest on alpha is stated precisely: the assumption-free lower bound (S0 -- the alpha-independent 1.22e-3 hydrogen-bonding term acting alone, no thermal-expansion coefficient assumed) flips 0 verdicts, so the H-bonding term BY ITSELF does not refute H0; thermal expansion is necessary. But the refutation is not sensitive to WHICH alpha: invariance already breaks at alpha = 0.000261 1/K, far below any real organic liquid, and the common-alpha flip count is constant at [2] across the entire declared 6.0e-4 .. 1.5e-3 1/K range -- so no alpha value was tuned to produce the result. At alpha = 1.0e-3 1/K the RED of every pair scales between 0.975033 and 1.034313 of its 25 C value while the rank ordering is essentially preserved (min Kendall tau-b 0.990631, AUROC in [0.94733, 0.952381]) -- i.e. temperature moves the DECISION BOUNDARY, not the ranking. Mechanism: co-scaling R with the polymer's own Hansen-norm contraction (S3) leaves 0 flips, removing 5, which localises the drift to the temperature-invariant R rather than to the parameter law. Practical consequence for solvent selection: a fixed-R 25 C Hansen sphere is systematically more permissive at tropical application temperature and more restrictive in winter application, and the exposure is confined to pairs sitting within a few percent of RED = 1 -- e.g. polyamide-nylon66::dimethylformamide (RED 0.9868 at 25 C) crosses the boundary at 12.13 C and polystyrene::acetone (RED 1.0053) crosses at 29.52 C. Effect size is deliberately not overstated: the paired pinned-seed bootstrap CIs on the change in agreement with the 25 C label set are [-0.04, 0.0] at 5 C and [-0.04, 0.0] at 40 C, both straddling zero, so at n = 75 the AGGREGATE divergence is not resolvable -- the durable finding is the identified boundary-crossing pairs and the fixed-R mechanism, not a headline accuracy change. Note also that the fixture labels are 25 C observations, so off-25 C counts measure divergence from the shipped verdict set, not predictive error. Scope: self-authored CC0 fixture, LOCKED_INTERNAL, CONSTRAINT_ONLY -- this is a self-consistency falsification of the shipped rule, NOT external validation, and it does not re-label any pair.",
  "label_temperature_caveat": {
    "fixture_labels_observed_at_c": 25.0,
    "note": "observed_compatible labels are 25 C observations, so the correct_of_75 counts reported at other temperatures measure DIVERGENCE of the temperature-corrected verdict from the 25 C label set, NOT predictive error at that temperature. No pair is re-labelled and no accuracy claim is made off 25 C."
  },
